ages and these refer  to sets of uh  rearrangements that are believed to be  related by descent  but differ owing to somatic  hypermutation and class switch  recombination  and through uh through a number of  studies we and others have demonstrated  that this assay  is able to detect clones at 10 to the  negative 6 limit of detection  so here i have an example of one such  study  here we we took genomic dna from a b  cell line ca46 and spiked it  in to peripheral blood leukocyte genomic  dna  at a frequency of 10 to the negative six  so two picograms of cell line dna  per two micrograms of genomic dna  and from this large stock of material  then we prepared  30 libraries of two micrograms each and  sequenced each  using the ighsr assay now  two picograms of genomic dna is  approximately one-thirds  cell equivalent and so in an optimal  situation you might  expect to detect the rear the  rearrangement at a frequency  uh or of of one in every three libraries  and in fact we we detected in 10 out of  the 30 libraries  very close to expectation and from this  data you can  back calculate the number of libraries  needed to obtain  a 95 or 99 uh  probability of detection at 10 to  negative 6 and this fits very closely  what  with what others have observed in the  fields so this really highlights  the reason why the field has been  gravitating towards  immune repertoire sequencing for the  rare phone detection application  now the the other application i'd like  to discuss is  a somatic hyper mutation analysis within  the context of chronic lymphocytic  leukemia  and it's been established that the level  of somatic hypermutation of the variable  gene  is an as that can help  predict the outcome of that cancer type  cll having a vg mutation of greater than  two percent  uh sometimes referred to as mcl tends to  have a better prognosis than than cll  that  has an unmutated variable gene or less  than two percent somatic hypermutation  with that said this is an imperfect  pro