 for each variable gene  a roughly gaussian distribution of band  intensity  where each band corresponds to a  different cbr through the nucleotide  length  now in an individual with an abnormality  either autoimmune disease or otherwise  the  the the plots would deviate from this  gaussian distribution  and on panel b of this of this slide i  have an example of uh  myasthenia gravis this is a t cell  mediated autoimmune disorder  where you're observing a non-gaussian  distribution  now i set this as context because with  ngs data we can take this type of  analysis to uh  to a higher level and here i'd like to  introduce the  the spectra typing plots within the ion  reporter companion analysis  for these assays so here you're looking  at a spectrotyping analysis  from the ighlr assay  and in this plot the rearrangements  detected in a sample are displayed from  left to right across the plot by the  variable gene identity  and along the y-axis by the cdr3  nucleotide length  the size of the circle indicates the  frequency of a particular v-gene  cdr3 nucleotide length combination and  the coloring indicates the level of  somatic hypermutation  so you can note there's a lot of  information in a single plot  and with within ion reporter these plots  are interactive  so i can scroll my cursor over that plot  and bring up extra information  at any position so for example  at the at the noted location i've now  brought up a pop-up window  which indicates the v gene is v323  the cdr3 nucleotide length is 48 base  pairs  the largest clone at this position has a  frequency of about 1  and with a vg mutation of 13.7  so what i'm showing you here is a  healthy individual  a polyclonal repertoire and you can note  there are many circles in this plot that  are about the same size  but now let's take a look at an  individual with cll  so here it's quite obvious the  difference between the  the previous sample uh one can observe a  dominating clone  uh that dwarfs the size of the 