ry table again within ion reporter  this provides a breakdown  of the features of each clonal lineage  and so the lineage id number one the  most  abundant clonal lineage consists of two  clones expressing  igm and igd and having a combined  frequency of  nearly 80 percent of the repertoire so  clearly this is an abnormal sample  uh consistent with with cll  that was an easy case now let's take a  look at a more challenging case  this was described uh to me as  a case that was challenging to interpret  through sanger sequencing  owing to polyclonal backgrounds and you  can  you can observe why there's uh there's  really not a dominating clone  in the way that we saw with that  previous sample but here is where the  isotype information  can be useful and and this brings us to  the important part of the second  learning objective  with the isotype information we can  partition the repertoire  and that can help us hone in on an  abnormally  expanded clone so at the top i'm going  to click on igd  and when i do you can note there's a  dominating clone  while the other igd expressing b-cells  are quite rare  so this is abnormal and it suggests that  in fact the clone of  that's that's highlighted in this plot  uh corresponds to the cll and  and thus one can more easily uh  interpret uh the somatic mutation value  of that clone  and just by contrast here i'm going to  click on ig a1 and look at look at those  cells  and they are indeed polyclonal so you  can tell from this  analysis that the malignant clone is not  does not express iga1 that looks  uh looks normal  so now i'm going to switch into the the  third  part of this talk where i want to  describe the impact of somatic  hypermutation on clonality and clone  tracking  applications and to highlight the  importance of  of this phenomenon we're going to look  at a follicular lymphoma research sample  so to to get back to what we discussed  the beginning of this presentation these  cells  unlike t cells have additional