other  clones uh  the other parts of the of this plot and  you can note by the coloring  that this this clone likely has a level  of somatic hypermutation  greater than zero and by hovering over  it we can bring up additional  information  and here you'll note that  at this position in the repertoire the  largest clone has a frequency of 44  and a v gene percentage mutation of 8.3  percent  noted at the bottom of the pop-up so  within cll  this 8.3 beaching mutation would be  interpreted as a good sign  and so a potentially better prognosis  now you'll note at the top there are  buttons that may be  clicked by by the user in fact  with these plots we can highlight a  partition the repertoire by isotype  such that we're only viewing  rearrangements having a particular item  type so now  instead of viewing the entire repertoire  i'm going to click on igm  and in doing so uh i again see this  dominating uh  a circle corresponding to the the  malignant clone  which suggests that the clone express is  igm  now i'm going to click on igd  and here you see it again so this this  cll clone  must express igm and ige potentially as  the product of  uh alternative splicing now the  the same in the information that we just  observed through spec  the spectrotyping analysis is also found  within the clone submarine tables of ion  reporter  these are searchable sortable tables  and each row of this table shows the  features of  a different clone so you'll note that  the first two rows of this table  corresponds to the two clones that we  just observed  one expressing igm and one expressing  igd  and these have the same level of  mutation of 8.3  and to the leftmost portion of this  table you can note a column that  indicates  the lineage id the software will  automatically assign clones to clonal  lineages  uh based on on the features of that  rearrangement  so you can see that the first two clones  are believed to be related by descent  and if we take a look at the lineage  summary table again within ion reporter  this provides a breakdown  of the features of each clonal lineage  and so the lineage id number one the  most  abundant clonal lineage consists of two  clones expressing  igm and igd and having a combined  frequency of  nearly 80 percent of the repertoire so  clearly this is an abnormal sample  uh consistent with with cll  that was an easy case now let's take a  look at a more challenging case  this was described uh to me as  a case that was challenging to interpret  through sanger sequencing  owing to polyclonal backgrounds and you  can  you can observe why there's uh there's  really not a dominating clone  in the way that we saw with that  previous sample but here is where the  isotype information  can be useful and and this brings us to  the important part of the second  learning objective  with the isotype information we can  partition the repertoire  and that can help us hone in on an  abnormally  expanded clone so at the top i'm going  to click on igd  and when i do you can note there's a  dominating clone  while the other igd expressing b-cells  are quite rare  so this is abnormal and it suggests that  in fact the clone of  that's that's highlighted in this plot  uh corresponds to the cll and  and thus one can more easily uh  interpret uh the somatic mutation value  of that clone  and just by contrast here i'm going to  click on ig a1 and look at look at those  cells  and they are indeed polyclonal so you  can tell from this  analysis that the malignant clone is not  does not express iga1 that looks  uh looks normal  so now i'm going to switch into the the  third  part of this talk where i want to  describe the impact of somatic  hypermutation on clonality and clone  tracking  applications and to highlight the  importance of  of this phenomenon we're going to look  at a follicular lymphoma research sample  so to to get back to what we discussed  the beginning of this presentation these  cells  unlike t cells have additional  diversification  mechanisms after vdj recombination where  they can undergo somatic hypermutation  and class switch recombination the  somatic hypermutation  introduces essentially point mutations  within the vdj region  which can give rise to any number of  subclones  now in cases where a b cell malignancy  of interest  is undergoing constant somatic  hypermutation  one could have a situation where there  are many subclones  uh that that one may wish to detect and  and  and track by by sequencing  so here's an example of a case  a sample of suspected follicular  lymphoma  and this again was a sample that was  difficult to evaluate for clonality  because as you can note there's not  a clearly dominating clone and this  data was produced by the igh sr assay  from ffpe dna  but with follicular lymphoma it's it's  possible that  the malignant clone could undergo  on could demonstrate ongoing somatic  hypermutation which could give rise to  many subclones  in that case we may observe an excess of  clones  having a particular variable gene cdr3  nucleotide length combination  and i can actually change the coloring  of this plot to highlight  the number of clones within the  repertoire by clicking this button  and if this is the case we may see an  excess of clones then  in one part of the repertoire and  doing so uh you know  with this type of analysis you can you  can really ask  whether this sample it truly is clonal  or whether it's something more benign  like molecular hyperplasia  so uh by clicking that button you can  note that  there is something unusual here one part  of the repertoire has an excess of  clones as we suspected  and this may be consistent with  follicular lymphoma and the information  displayed on this plot is also shown  within  the lineage summary table and you can  take a look at  the first row of this table lineage id  number one  there are actually 38 clones within this  clonal lineage  while the others have one or two so this  is really an  excess number of clones consistent with  ongoing somatic hypermutation creating  this  rich subclonal structure and  without a clonal lineage analysis this  type of feature would be missed because  each of these sub phones individually is  at low frequency  but together they create the signal that  that is that that helps one understand  uh the nature of the sample so from this  i i want to convey that clonal lineage  analysis  uh is an essential part of interpreting  the b-cell repertoire  so with that i i'm going to finish the  talk  to summarize i described b-cell  repertoire sequencing  and and how it has emerged as an  attractive tool for hemato oncology  research  this is owing to the great limit of  detection  the ability to track clones but also  subclones  and then i also noted how it differs  from traditional approaches like  cytometry in that it requires a  pre-treatment or diagnostic sample  i i i discussed spectra typing as a tool  to  interpret the repertoire and further  discuss  how isotype inflammation can help  dissemble ua certain cll cases  where one wishes to analyze the somatic  type mutation  and then i i ended with a a a case  study highlighting the need to identify  clonal lineages  within the b-cell repertoire data and  how that can really help facilitate the  interpretation of the repertoire so with  that i'd like to  thank you for your time and uh really  happy to  take any further questions that you  might have  thank you
 

